1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,973
Most of us met the determinant
as a formula: a d minus b c.

2
00:00:05,773 --> 00:00:09,976
It tells us whether a system of linear
equations has a unique solution.

3
00:00:11,376 --> 00:00:16,923
It measures how much a linear transformation
scales area. Here, every area doubles.

4
00:00:18,323 --> 00:00:21,181
And when it's zero,
everything gets squashed flat.

5
00:00:21,981 --> 00:00:24,499
It even finds the area of any polygon.

6
00:00:26,899 --> 00:00:31,778
One of the most beautiful and unexpected
places I've found determinants is

7
00:00:31,778 --> 00:00:32,701
combinatorics.

8
00:00:33,501 --> 00:00:36,765
And today, I want to show you
how determinants can count.

9
00:00:38,565 --> 00:00:40,485
Let's start with a simple puzzle.

10
00:00:42,465 --> 00:00:43,830
Meet these two ants.

11
00:00:44,630 --> 00:00:46,678
They both live in a grid-like world.

12
00:00:47,478 --> 00:00:50,081
And they both want to
get back home, right now.

13
00:00:50,881 --> 00:00:56,193
On this grid there are rules. An ant can
only step up, or to the right. Never down,

14
00:00:56,193 --> 00:00:57,153
and never left.

15
00:00:57,953 --> 00:01:02,934
Of course, there are many paths home. The
blue ant could go this way, or this way.

16
00:01:02,934 --> 00:01:05,484
And the orange ant, this way, or that way.

17
00:01:06,284 --> 00:01:10,654
But today, they can't just take any
path. They had a fight, and if they meet,

18
00:01:10,654 --> 00:01:12,300
they'll start fighting again.

19
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:17,998
So neither will set foot on any dot the
other one walks on. Not even later: ants

20
00:01:17,998 --> 00:01:19,222
leave a scent trail.

21
00:01:20,022 --> 00:01:23,521
So, how many pairs of paths get
them home without intersecting?

22
00:01:24,321 --> 00:01:29,326
You can always find one easily. Here,
the blue one goes up and around, and the

23
00:01:29,326 --> 00:01:32,534
orange one goes right and
around. They never meet.

24
00:01:33,334 --> 00:01:35,020
And here's one where they cross.

25
00:01:35,820 --> 00:01:40,898
Let's check them all, one by one. This
pair works. This one doesn't. Neither does

26
00:01:40,898 --> 00:01:42,966
this one. Or this one. And so on.

27
00:01:48,266 --> 00:01:50,741
Twenty pairs never meet, and sixteen do.

28
00:01:53,323 --> 00:01:58,234
I know what you're thinking: this is stupid.
There must be an easier way. Well, here

29
00:01:58,234 --> 00:01:59,403
comes an old friend.

30
00:02:00,203 --> 00:02:05,628
Consider this two-by-two matrix, A. Its
rows are the ants, and its columns are the

31
00:02:05,628 --> 00:02:06,091
houses.

32
00:02:06,891 --> 00:02:11,734
The first entry counts the paths from the
blue ant to its own home. There are six.

33
00:02:13,534 --> 00:02:18,227
The second entry is a little sneakier:
the paths from the blue ant to the orange

34
00:02:18,227 --> 00:02:19,870
ant's house. There are four.

35
00:02:21,670 --> 00:02:27,009
And by symmetry, the orange ant gives the
same numbers the other way round: four,

36
00:02:27,009 --> 00:02:27,536
and six.

37
00:02:28,336 --> 00:02:32,944
Now find the determinant of A: six
times six, minus four times four,

38
00:02:33,744 --> 00:02:35,472
which is twenty. Voilà!

39
00:02:37,272 --> 00:02:38,894
The same number as before.

40
00:02:41,289 --> 00:02:44,233
In fact, there's nothing
special about this scenario.

41
00:02:45,033 --> 00:02:48,937
Make the grid bigger, and bring in a
third ant, with a house of its own.

42
00:02:49,737 --> 00:02:55,016
Build the same kind of matrix: three ants,
three houses, and each entry counts the

43
00:02:55,016 --> 00:02:57,076
paths from one ant to one house.

44
00:02:57,876 --> 00:02:59,582
Its determinant is fifty.

45
00:03:00,382 --> 00:03:05,516
And fifty is exactly the number of ways
all three ants can get home without any of

46
00:03:05,516 --> 00:03:07,081
their paths intersecting.

47
00:03:14,856 --> 00:03:16,371
So, why does this work?

48
00:03:17,171 --> 00:03:19,773
Let's get back to our two
ants, and work it out.

49
00:03:22,156 --> 00:03:27,416
Here's the thing that bugged me. Six times
six counts all thirty-six pairs going

50
00:03:27,416 --> 00:03:27,745
home.

51
00:03:29,145 --> 00:03:31,407
Twenty never meet. Sixteen do.

52
00:03:33,007 --> 00:03:37,913
Four times four counts the pairs where
the ants swap houses: also sixteen.

53
00:03:39,513 --> 00:03:44,388
So for the determinant to give twenty,
the minus sign must cancel the sixteen

54
00:03:44,388 --> 00:03:47,364
meeting pairs against
the sixteen swapped ones.

55
00:03:48,764 --> 00:03:51,687
So they must match up,
one to one. Let's see how.

56
00:03:54,456 --> 00:03:56,653
Take a pair where the ants swap houses.

57
00:03:59,653 --> 00:04:03,835
The blue ant starts to the left of
the orange one, and ends to its right.

58
00:04:06,835 --> 00:04:08,861
So somewhere, they must share a dot.

59
00:04:10,261 --> 00:04:13,568
Take the first shared dot,
and swap the tails from there.

60
00:04:15,368 --> 00:04:18,611
Now each ant is at its own
home, and their paths meet.

61
00:04:24,189 --> 00:04:27,346
Now the other way: a pair
that meets on the way home.

62
00:04:28,946 --> 00:04:33,533
Swap the tails at the first shared dot,
and they end up in each other's houses.

63
00:04:35,333 --> 00:04:38,640
Swap again at the same dot, and
we're back where we started.

64
00:04:41,422 --> 00:04:44,729
So swapped pairs and meeting
pairs match up, one to one.

65
00:04:45,929 --> 00:04:48,297
This meeting pair goes
with this swapped pair.

66
00:04:50,697 --> 00:04:52,147
This one, with this one.

67
00:04:54,547 --> 00:04:56,169
And this one, with this one.

68
00:04:58,169 --> 00:04:59,150
And so on.

69
00:05:04,450 --> 00:05:05,623
Sixteen of each.

70
00:05:08,221 --> 00:05:09,480
Now, the formula.

71
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:16,498
Six times six counts every pair going home:
twenty that never meet, and sixteen that

72
00:05:16,498 --> 00:05:16,832
meet.

73
00:05:18,632 --> 00:05:21,640
Four times four counts
the sixteen swapped pairs.

74
00:05:23,440 --> 00:05:25,680
They cancel the meeting pairs exactly.

75
00:05:27,280 --> 00:05:28,474
Leaving twenty.

76
00:05:30,274 --> 00:05:34,605
A bijective proof: the only kind
a combinatorialist fully trusts.

77
00:05:37,188 --> 00:05:40,580
So far, ants on a grid.
Now, the general lemma.

78
00:05:42,955 --> 00:05:48,242
Martin Aigner and Günter Ziegler begin
their chapter on this lemma like this: The

79
00:05:48,242 --> 00:05:53,660
essence of mathematics is proving theorems,
and so, that is what mathematicians do:

80
00:05:53,660 --> 00:05:54,966
they prove theorems.

81
00:05:55,766 --> 00:06:00,401
But to tell the truth, what they really
want to prove, once in their lifetime, is a

82
00:06:00,401 --> 00:06:00,736
Lemma.

83
00:06:02,336 --> 00:06:04,384
And this is one of those lemmas.

84
00:06:06,755 --> 00:06:09,550
It's called the
Lindström–Gessel–Viennot lemma.

85
00:06:10,350 --> 00:06:14,936
Bernt Lindström found it
in 1973, in matroid theory.

86
00:06:15,736 --> 00:06:22,154
Ira Gessel and Xavier Viennot rediscovered
it in 1985, and showed how much it can

87
00:06:22,154 --> 00:06:22,392
do.

88
00:06:24,988 --> 00:06:30,343
Take any one-way network: roads you can
only travel in one direction, with no loops.

89
00:06:31,743 --> 00:06:36,915
Pick some starting points, a one up to a
n, and the same number of ends, b one up to

90
00:06:36,915 --> 00:06:37,161
b n.

91
00:06:38,561 --> 00:06:44,358
Build a matrix M: the entry in row i,
column j counts the paths from a i to b j.

92
00:06:44,358 --> 00:06:46,967
From a one to b two, there are four.

93
00:06:48,567 --> 00:06:54,295
Then the determinant of M counts the families
of paths, a one to b one, all the way

94
00:06:54,295 --> 00:06:56,503
to a n to b n, that never touch.

95
00:06:57,903 --> 00:07:03,280
As long as, like on our grid, any crossed
pairing is forced to touch. Here, the

96
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:07,908
determinant is thirteen: exactly
thirteen families that never touch.

97
00:07:10,687 --> 00:07:15,984
So any puzzle that is secretly a family of
paths that never touch, a determinant can

98
00:07:15,984 --> 00:07:16,362
count.

99
00:07:17,162 --> 00:07:19,466
Does the pattern repeat? Let's look.

100
00:07:22,054 --> 00:07:24,721
Take a hexagon, cut into little triangles.

101
00:07:26,121 --> 00:07:30,942
Glue two neighbours together and you get
a tile. Tiles point in three directions.

102
00:07:32,342 --> 00:07:38,046
Now cover the hexagon with tiles: no gaps,
no overlaps. Notice anything? It looks

103
00:07:38,046 --> 00:07:39,595
like a stack of boxes.

104
00:07:40,995 --> 00:07:44,963
It's completely flat. Your
brain is doing the 3D for free.

105
00:07:46,363 --> 00:07:48,091
How many ways can you tile it?

106
00:07:50,891 --> 00:07:55,627
Fade out the flat tiles. The others
link up into chains, from left to right.

107
00:07:57,227 --> 00:08:00,193
Tiles never overlap, so
the chains never touch.

108
00:08:01,593 --> 00:08:06,393
Now squash the hexagon onto our grid,
and the two chains become our two ants.

109
00:08:10,193 --> 00:08:14,694
So there are twenty tilings. All this
time, our ants were tiling a hexagon.

110
00:08:17,687 --> 00:08:22,188
Make the box bigger: three by three
by three, and stack the boxes in.

111
00:08:23,788 --> 00:08:26,220
Now there are three chains, so three ants.

112
00:08:27,620 --> 00:08:30,820
Squash it onto a grid:
three ants that never touch.

113
00:08:32,420 --> 00:08:37,615
Same recipe as before: count the paths
from each ant to each house, and take the

114
00:08:37,615 --> 00:08:38,394
determinant.

115
00:08:39,994 --> 00:08:42,703
Nine hundred and eighty stacks of boxes.

116
00:08:44,303 --> 00:08:49,887
Percy MacMahon counted these over a century
ago, with heavy algebra. Our ants do it

117
00:08:49,887 --> 00:08:51,300
with one determinant.

118
00:08:54,087 --> 00:08:59,422
Here's a mountain range: every step goes
up or down, and it never dips below sea

119
00:08:59,422 --> 00:09:02,023
level. It starts and ends at sea level.

120
00:09:03,623 --> 00:09:08,910
How many ranges are there? With two
steps, just one. With four steps, two. Six

121
00:09:08,910 --> 00:09:11,282
steps: five. Eight steps: fourteen.

122
00:09:12,882 --> 00:09:18,145
One, two, five, fourteen: these are the
Catalan numbers, one of the most famous

123
00:09:18,145 --> 00:09:19,943
sequences in combinatorics.

124
00:09:21,543 --> 00:09:27,078
They count many things. Read each up-step
as an opening bracket, and each down-step

125
00:09:27,078 --> 00:09:31,612
as a closing one: every range becomes
a balanced string of brackets.

126
00:09:33,212 --> 00:09:37,756
They also count the ways to cut a
pentagon into triangles: five again.

127
00:09:39,356 --> 00:09:43,580
Now draw two ranges, one inside the
other, and they must never touch.

128
00:09:45,180 --> 00:09:49,575
They're paths again: two walkers, starting
here and here, ending here and here.

129
00:09:50,975 --> 00:09:56,381
From the blue start to the blue end is
four steps: two ranges. To the orange end,

130
00:09:56,381 --> 00:10:00,319
six steps: five. The orange
walker gets five, and fourteen.

131
00:10:01,919 --> 00:10:07,302
So the matrix is made of Catalan numbers,
and its determinant, two times fourteen

132
00:10:07,302 --> 00:10:09,428
minus five times five, is three.

133
00:10:11,028 --> 00:10:13,162
And here they are: exactly three.

134
00:10:16,154 --> 00:10:21,990
And one more, a real beauty: the Aztec
diamond. Rows of two, four, six squares, and

135
00:10:21,990 --> 00:10:23,045
then back down.

136
00:10:24,445 --> 00:10:28,839
Cover it with dominoes. Each domino
covers two neighbouring squares.

137
00:10:30,439 --> 00:10:33,341
The smallest diamond can
be tiled in two ways.

138
00:10:34,741 --> 00:10:36,490
The next one, in eight ways.

139
00:10:37,890 --> 00:10:39,618
The next, in sixty-four.

140
00:10:41,018 --> 00:10:42,234
Guess the next one.

141
00:10:45,534 --> 00:10:51,166
One thousand and twenty-four. Powers of
two, every single time. That's suspicious.

142
00:10:53,954 --> 00:10:56,749
Here's the trick. Colour
the board like a chessboard.

143
00:10:58,149 --> 00:11:03,711
Now every domino is one of four kinds:
lying flat or standing up, and starting on a

144
00:11:03,711 --> 00:11:05,253
light or a dark square.

145
00:11:06,653 --> 00:11:11,517
Give each kind a job: a flat step, a
step up, a step down, or nothing at all.

146
00:11:13,117 --> 00:11:17,788
Put the steps together, and they link up
into paths, from the left side to the right

147
00:11:17,788 --> 00:11:18,066
side.

148
00:11:19,466 --> 00:11:25,066
Dominoes never overlap, so the paths never
touch. And every pair of paths that never

149
00:11:25,066 --> 00:11:27,466
touch comes from exactly one tiling.

150
00:11:29,066 --> 00:11:33,610
So, same recipe as before: count the
paths from each start to each end.

151
00:11:35,210 --> 00:11:40,074
Two times six, minus two times two:
eight. Exactly the eight tilings.

152
00:11:41,674 --> 00:11:46,261
One size up, there are three paths,
and a three-by-three determinant gives

153
00:11:46,261 --> 00:11:46,943
sixty-four.

154
00:11:48,543 --> 00:11:54,597
In general, the diamond of order n has two
to the power n times n plus one over two

155
00:11:54,597 --> 00:11:58,463
tilings. A determinant,
hiding in a pile of dominoes.

156
00:12:01,453 --> 00:12:06,934
Now pick one tiling at random, every tiling
equally likely, on a big diamond, and

157
00:12:06,934 --> 00:12:07,746
let it grow.

158
00:12:08,546 --> 00:12:09,997
This is order sixty.

159
00:12:10,797 --> 00:12:16,281
The corners freeze into brick walls. The
middle is chaos. And as the diamond grows,

160
00:12:16,281 --> 00:12:19,650
the boundary between them
becomes a perfect circle.

161
00:12:20,450 --> 00:12:22,520
It's called the arctic circle theorem.

162
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:29,381
But not every counting problem
is secretly paths. What then?

163
00:12:31,753 --> 00:12:36,981
Take dominoes on a chessboard. Dominoes
aren't paths, but there's a determinant

164
00:12:36,981 --> 00:12:41,481
trick here too. Start small: a
two-by-three board has three tilings.

165
00:12:42,281 --> 00:12:47,794
A domino joins a black square to a white
neighbour. Write a one wherever they touch.

166
00:12:47,794 --> 00:12:52,651
Each tiling picks one entry in every
row and every column: one term of the

167
00:12:52,651 --> 00:12:53,438
determinant.

168
00:12:54,238 --> 00:12:58,718
If determinants count pairings, this
should give three. It gives minus one.

169
00:12:59,518 --> 00:13:04,289
The terms come with signs: plus for
this one, minus for these two. Here the

170
00:13:04,289 --> 00:13:06,324
cancellation eats the good ones.

171
00:13:08,320 --> 00:13:14,861
In 1961 the physicist Pieter Kasteleyn
had an idea: if the signs don't cancel the

172
00:13:14,861 --> 00:13:17,365
way you want, change the signs.

173
00:13:18,165 --> 00:13:23,843
Put a minus sign on this one edge. Only the
all-vertical tiling uses it, so only its

174
00:13:23,843 --> 00:13:24,587
sign flips.

175
00:13:25,387 --> 00:13:31,174
Now every tiling carries the same sign.
The determinant is minus three. Ignore the

176
00:13:31,174 --> 00:13:32,021
sign: three.

177
00:13:34,020 --> 00:13:39,190
On a four-by-four board, the plain
determinant says there are zero tilings. Very

178
00:13:39,190 --> 00:13:40,612
confident, very wrong.

179
00:13:41,412 --> 00:13:46,806
Put minus signs on the vertical edges of
every other column, and the determinant

180
00:13:46,806 --> 00:13:48,964
gives thirty-six. Exactly right.

181
00:13:50,953 --> 00:13:56,293
Now the full chessboard: twelve million,
nine hundred eighty-eight thousand, eight

182
00:13:56,293 --> 00:14:01,176
hundred sixteen tilings. Listing one
per second would take five months. The

183
00:14:01,176 --> 00:14:03,390
determinant does it in an instant.

184
00:14:04,190 --> 00:14:09,578
The rule behind it: every little loop of
four edges carries an odd number of minus

185
00:14:09,578 --> 00:14:09,972
signs.

186
00:14:10,772 --> 00:14:16,366
And it's not really about chessboards.
Squares are vertices, dominoes are edges, and

187
00:14:16,366 --> 00:14:18,430
a tiling is a perfect matching.

188
00:14:19,230 --> 00:14:24,801
Kasteleyn, Temperley and Fisher made this
work for any graph you can draw without

189
00:14:24,801 --> 00:14:30,577
crossings: the FKT algorithm counts perfect
matchings of planar graphs in polynomial

190
00:14:30,577 --> 00:14:30,921
time.

191
00:14:31,721 --> 00:14:37,020
When a physicist needs to count twelve
million tilings, they don't count. They take

192
00:14:37,020 --> 00:14:40,404
a determinant, and then let
the board go to infinity.

193
00:14:42,386 --> 00:14:48,271
Let's step back. Ants that never meet.
Tilings of a hexagon. Boxes in a corner.

194
00:14:48,271 --> 00:14:51,623
Mountain ranges. Aztec
diamonds. Chessboards.

195
00:14:52,423 --> 00:14:54,663
All counted by one determinant each.

196
00:14:56,653 --> 00:15:01,770
And it's the same trick every time. Count
everything, the families that meet and the

197
00:15:01,770 --> 00:15:03,842
ones that don't, each with a sign.

198
00:15:04,642 --> 00:15:09,727
The meeting ones pair up with opposite
signs, and cancel. What's left is exactly

199
00:15:09,727 --> 00:15:10,744
what you wanted.

200
00:15:11,544 --> 00:15:17,929
For a computer scientist, that's remarkable.
Counting is usually hopeless: counting

201
00:15:17,929 --> 00:15:24,237
perfect matchings of a general graph is
sharp-P complete. That's Valiant, in 1979.

202
00:15:25,037 --> 00:15:30,392
But if your puzzle turns into non-touching
paths, or a matching on a planar graph,

203
00:15:30,392 --> 00:15:33,528
it becomes one determinant,
and polynomial time.

204
00:15:34,328 --> 00:15:37,848
Determinants can count,
because minus signs can cancel.

205
00:15:41,319 --> 00:15:46,658
As for our ants: they can finally go home
in peace. Twenty ways home, and they never

206
00:15:46,658 --> 00:15:47,420
had to meet.

207
00:15:49,720 --> 00:15:51,043
Thanks for watching.

